condition_name: Intra-hepatic Cholestatic Jaundice of Pregnancy
condition_slug: intra-hepatic-cholestatic-jaundice-of-pregnancy
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "Also termed 'recurrent obstructive jaundice' or 'idiopathic cholestasis'. Thought to be due to the effect of high estrogen levels on the liver, which results in decreased conjugation of bilirubin, A rare condition. Incidence of 1:500 pregnancies, More commonly seen in among Scandinavians Its exact aetiology is unknown"
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: Clinical Features
    features[4]: Intense pruritus due to retention of bile salts,The most common presenting symptom and may occur in the absence of other symptoms,Onset of symptoms usually in the third trimester,Jaundice is not often seen
investigations[6]: Bilirubinuria,Elevated bile acids,Elevated alkaline phosphatase,Elevated liver transferase enzymes,Prothrombin time,"Always exclude viral disease, gallstones and treatment with chlorpromazine"
treatment:
  goals[0]:
  non_drug[2]: Careful maternal follow-up with LFTs,"Foetal surveillance: by growth (serial USS biometry) and well-being (CTG) monitoring"
  drug[0]:
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[0]:
  supportive_measures[3]: "If all is well, induce at 38 weeks",Management of pruritus,See cholestasis of pregnancy
differential_diagnoses[0]:
complications[8]: Maternal Haemorrhage,Preterm labour,Steatorrhea,Foetal distress,Still-birth,Perinatal death,Prematurity and its problems,Meconium staining of the liquor
prevention[0]:
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null